 an expression equivalent to $\\dfrac 27\\, {(y-3)}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45b1ee7d-b7ce-5336-9d2a-1a5953310026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105485 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing $y-3$ as $\\dfrac{y-3}{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7}\\,(y-3) & =\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot (y-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{y-3}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (y-3)}{7\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y-6}{7}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105486 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105486 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an expression that's equivalent to $\\dfrac{2}3 a.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53825e9f-1b2f-582e-ad91-50970995d523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105486 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing $a$ as $\\dfrac{a}{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\,a & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot a \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{a}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot a}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2a}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expressions $\\dfrac{2}{3}a$ and $\\dfrac{2a}{3}$ are equivalent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105487 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1544" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105487 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an expression equivalent to $\\dfrac{2(3-x)}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c24fff9-3d43-53f3-8c87-daacb675f802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105487 #edb/ref 1074 "Pulling out the variable part, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(3-x)}{5} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (3-x)}{5\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{3-x}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot (3-x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(3-x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105683 #edb/ref 1069 "e-832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105683 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an  expression  equivalent to $-\\dfrac{2}9 (-z).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105683 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9062b933-10d8-5f52-b41e-c528d35c9fe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105683 #edb/ref 1074 "First, remember that the product of two negative numbers is a positive number. Thus, \n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2}9 (-z) = \\dfrac{2}{9} z.\n$$\n\nNow, writing $z$ as $\\dfrac{z}{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}z & =\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot z \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot \\frac{z}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot z}{9\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2z}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expressions $-\\dfrac{2}9 (-z)$ and $\\dfrac{2z}{9}$ are equivalent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Equivalent Expressions with Negative Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105835 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cd86e42a-deb3-5f59-bbbc-f096a1f368aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Equivalent Expressions with Binomial Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105910 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "796da155-1e8c-5f6c-896f-2dce9a678f5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105911 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049253 #edb/ref 175921860